# Trace restored-root cache selection

The scratch GitHub witness passes one selected Native case. Its compiler
passes. Two other cases were filtered out. The production tree equals
frozen a7d22d7c; only the added scratch test differs. No candidate source
or dependency changed, and no package was installed.

The actual credential access reads GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY twice after
root removal and twice after restoration. worker.ts reads it once while
building its cache tuple. github-wiring.ts reads it once in readiness,
which runs only when the factory builds an adapter. The unchanged third
access reads it once and therefore reuses the adapter. The probe counts
property names only; it never records the private-key value. Its policy
check runs after the credential access, so it cannot warm that access.

The removed root denies access and leaves live plaintext in custody. The
restored list permits one read. The unchanged third read finds no
plaintext. Both earlier accesses reach the real adapter through a real
membership-issued session. The observed list strings differ at restore.

At worker.ts wiring(), the map stores only the latest tuple per host.
Both register settings occupy that tuple. Its equality check compares
these string values. A different list makes the comparison fail, calls
the factory synchronously, and replaces the tuple and adapter together.
No await lies between tuple capture and installed-root parsing. The
helper parses a fresh frozen root array. For the fixture's two stable
list strings, the cache cannot reuse its removed-root adapter on restore.
The runtime trace witnesses this construction boundary directly.

The earlier stale-cache control removed the root setting from the tuple.
Both provider cases then answered 200 at root removal, where 403 was
required. Its original sealed control record remains in the focused
manifest; this probe does not repeat that control or relabel it as a pass.

These facts establish the synchronous cache transition under the tested
stable settings. They do not identify the original Gate3 refusal as a
session refusal or a custody refusal: that run did not record the
boundary. Its 403 remains unexplained. A passing Gate4 does not itself
close this finding. The planner must assess whether this trace and
source argument satisfy ruling f115404f before review proceeds.
